he second stimulus. Plots of fluorescence intensity without normalization\nL370@P3: are shown in Extended Data Fig. 7. c, Regression curves in the logistic regression analyses of Fres at lateral areas (leftmost), Fres at central areas (second\nL371@P3: left), Fmax at lateral areas (second right) and Fmax at central areas (rightmost). Leaf states (black dots: 1, closed; 0, open), regression curve (red lines), 95%\nL372@P3: CI of the regression curve (grey regions), predicted putative thresholds of Ft\nL373@P3:  to trigger closure of 50% leaves (solid blue lines) and 95% CI of the putative\nL374@P3: threshold (dashed blue lines) are shown. d,e, Decay curves of the fluorescence intensity (Ft\nL375@P3: ) after F1 is reached in the lateral (d) and central (e) area. The\nL376@P3: mean (black line) and standard deviation of the mean (grey region) are plotted every 0.02 s (n= 6, leaves from two biologically independent plants). Fres\nL377@P3: (magenta) and Fmax (green) in c are plotted. Leaves were closed (circles) or not (x). f, Regression curves in the logistic regression analyses of duration\nL378@P3: between the first and second stimuli. Leaf states (black dots: 1, closed; 0, open), regression curve (red lines), 95% CI of the regression curve (grey regions),\nL379@P3: predicted putative thresholds of Ft\nL380@P3:  to trigger closure of 50% leaves (solid blue lines) and 95% CI of the putative threshold (dashed blue lines) are shown.\nL381@P3: Table 1 | Putative thresholds of [Ca2+]cyt and duration with logistic regression analyses\nL382@P3: Explanatory variable Area Threshold (95% CI)a Odds ratio (95% CI)b Maximum likelihood Likelihood ratio test\nL383@P3: Fres Lateral 0.5058\nL384@P3: (0.3738–0.6598)\nL385@P3: 1.165 × 104 (1.32 × 102–1.145 × 107) 2.2 × 10−4 0.000002***\nL386@P3: Central 0.3756\nL387@P3: (0.2599–0.4999)\nL388@P3: 4.114 × 104\nL389@P3: (2.141 × 102–1.382 × 108)\nL390@P3: 9.023 × 10−5 0.000008***\nL391@P3: Fmax Lateral 1.327 (1.226–1.422) 7.627 × 104\nL392@P3: (1.558 × 102–7.536 × 108)\nL393@P3: 8.301 × 10−6 0.000044***\nL394@P3: Central 1.179 (1.026–1.364) 1.608 × 104 (0.932–3.825 × 109) 6.17 × 10−9 0.063311\nL395@P3: Duration – 30.88 s (21.4–45.42) 0.8917 (0.8093–0.9446) 3.229 × 10−4 0.000001***\nL396@P3: a\nL397@P3: Value of Fmax or Fres where half number of the trap leaves close. bEffect size calculated from the regression coefficient. a,b95% CI: predicted 95% confidence intervals of putative threshold. ***P< 0.001\nL398@P3: (n= 30).\nL399@P3-4: Nature Plants | www.nature.com/natureplantsNature Plants Letters\nL400@P4: when a leaf is removed from a stem at the attached position and\nL401@P4: the basal part of a petiole was immediately put in water. Even with\nL402@P4: no mechanical stimuli, the [Ca2+]cyt signal increased two or three\nL403@P4: times and the leaf closed (Extended Data Fig. 9, Supplementary\nL404@P4: Fig. 6 and Supplementary Video 7). Before the leaf closure, [Ca2+]cyt\nL405@P4: signals reached within the 95% CIs of the putative threshold\nL406@P4: (Extended Data Fig. 9). As the second experiment, we immersed\nL407@P4: a whole leaf in the calcium channel blocker La3+ solution9,11 to\nL408@P4: reduce [Ca2+]cyt levels. Although we do not know the reason, after\nL409@P4: 30min of immersion with no mechanical stimuli, fluorescence\nL410@P4: intensity increased at the marginal area of the leaf blade, where Ca2+\nL411@P4: waves caused by a mechanical stimulus to the sensory hair did not\nL412@P4: reach (Supplementary Fig. 7). In a whole leaf immersed in water\nL413@P4: as a control, the second mechanical stimulus raised [Ca2+]cyt over\nL414@P4: the putative threshold to close the leaf (Extended Data Fig. 10a,\nL415@P4: Supplementary Fig. 8a and Supplementary Video 8). On the other\nL416@P4: hand, LaCl3-treated leaves did not move even after the third stim\u0002ulus and [Ca2+]cyt did not reach 95% CI of the putative threshold\nL417@P4: at the lateral area (Extended Data Fig. 10b, Supplementary Fig. 8b\nL418@P4: and Supplementary Video 9). These results are concordant with the\nL419@P4: hypothesis that leaf movement is triggered when the [Ca2+]cyt sur\u0002passes the threshold for the movement.\nL420@P4: This study shows that (1) the first mechanical stimulus to a sen\u0002sory hair increases [Ca2+]cyt in the leaf tissue responsible for move\u0002ment, (2) the elevated [Ca2+]cyt decreases two-phase exponentially\nL421@P4: after the first stimulus, (3) the second stimulus additively increases\nL422@P4: [Ca2+]cyt, (4) [Ca2+]cyt meets a putative threshold for movement after\nL423@P4: the second stimulus and (5) the time course of [Ca2+]cyt decay after\nL424@P4: the first stimulus and the putative [Ca2+]cyt threshold for movement\nL425@P4: are consistent with a memory system in Dionaea. Further studies\nL426@P4: on Dionaea leaf movement will reveal the molecular mechanisms\nL427@P4: of calcium dynamics to trigger movement especially on the rela\u0002tionships of [Ca2+]cyt change to mechanostimulus, action potentials\nL428@P4: and turgor changes to trigger movement, as well as the evolution\nL429@P4: of rapid movement in plants. In addition, since the leaf area with\nL430@P4: increased [Ca2+]cyt corresponds to the area in which the effective\nL431@P4: quantum yield of photosystem II transiently decreases after the\nL432@P4: mechanical stimulus to the sensory hair22,23, studies on the relation\u0002ships between [Ca2+]cyt dynamics and other physiological responses\nL433@P4: will give insight into the evolution of carnivory in plants.\nL434@P4: Methods\nL435@P4: Plant materials and culture conditions. Seeds of Dionaea were kindly provided\nL436@P4: by J. Mundy (University of Copenhagen, Denmark)24 and sterilized in 80% (v/v)\nL437@P4: EtOH for 1min, 1% (w/v) benzalkonium chloride for 5min and 1% (w/v) sodium\nL438@P4: hypochlorite for 5min. Te seeds were rinsed three times in water and were sown on\nL439@P4: the half-strength Murashige and Skoog (MS) medium containing 3% (w/v) sucrose,\nL440@P4: 1×Gamborg’s vitamins, 0.1% (w/v) 2-(N-morpholino)ethanesulfonic acid, 0.05%\nL441@P4: (v/v) Plant Preservative Mixture (Plant Cell Technology) and 0.3% (w/v) phytagel.\nL442@P4: Te seeds were incubated at 25°C in continuous light until germination. Plants were\nL443@P4: cultured on modifed cocultivation medium15 (half-strength basal MS salts (1/2 MS;\nL444@P4: Wako) medium with 2% (w/v) sucrose, 1×Gamborg’s vitamins (Sigma-Aldrich),\nL445@P4: 0.1% (w/v) 2-(N-morpholino)ethanesulfonic acid (Dojindo Laboratories) at pH6.1)\nL446@P4: with 0.3% (w/v) phytagel (Sigma-Aldrich) in a tissue culture vessel (CUL-JAR300;\nL447@P4: AGC Techno Glass) under continuous light conditions at 25°C. For transformation,\nL448@P4: Dionaea plants cultivated under continuous light conditions were moved to\nL449@P4: continuous dark conditions and cultivated for 1–6 months to collect etiolated leaves.\nL450@P4: Transformation. To generate transgenic Dionaea constitutively expressing sGFP or\nL451@P4: GCaMP6f, the superbinary acceptor vector pSB1 and the superbinary intermediate\nL452@P4: vector pSB11 harbouring the sGFP or GCaMP6f sequence13,16,25 were individually\nL453@P4: introduced into LBA4404 Agrobacterium26. Agrobacterium was cultivated on solid\nL454@P4: LB medium (Sigma-Aldrich) supplemented with 1.5% (w/v) agar (Nacalai Tesque)\nL455@P4: and 50μgml–1 (w/v) of hygromycin-B (Life Technologies) for 48h at 30 °C. A single\nL456@P4: Agrobacterium colony was inoculated into 5ml of liquid LB medium with 25μgml–\nL457@P4: 1\nL458@P4:  (w/v) of hygromycin-B and precultured at 28 °C for 24h with shaking at 180 r.p.m.\nL459@P4: Then, a portion of Agrobacterium was diluted to a concentration of optical density\nL460@P4: OD600 0.15 with 40ml of liquid LB medium supplemented with 25μgml–1 of\nL461@P4: hygromycin-B in a 200-ml baffled flask and further cultivated at 28 °C with shaking\nL462@P4: at 180 r.p.m. until an OD600 of ~0.55 was reached. After centrifugation at 5,000g for\nL463@P4: 10min at 25 °C, the supernatant was removed and precipitated Agrobacterium cells\nL464@P4: were resuspended to OD600 0.3 in the modified cocultivation medium mentioned\nL465@P4: above with 40μgml–1 of acetosyringone (Wako) after washing with the modified\nL466@P4: cocultivation medium once. Etiolated leaves were cut at the middle of the petiole,\nL467@P4: submerged in 10ml of the Agrobacterium suspension and placed twice successively\nL468@P4: under a vacuum (−0.08MPa in gauge pressure) for 10min. The infected leaves\nL469@P4: were submerged in the modified cocultivation medium with fresh Agrobacterium\nL470@P4: and cultivated under continuous darkness at 25 °C for 72h. Agrobacterium-infected\nL471@P4: leaves were transplanted onto the modified cocultivation medium with 0.3% (w/v)\nL472@P4: phytagel and 150μgml–1 of cefotaxime sodium salt (SANOFI) and cultivated under\nL473@P4: continuous darkness at 25 °C. The solid medium was exchanged once a week for\nL474@P4: at least a month, until transgenic shoots formed. One month after infection, the\nL475@P4: leaves were transferred to solid 1/2 MS medium plates lacking cefotaxime sodium\nL476@P4: salt. Transgenic shoots were cultivated under 8h light/16h darkness at 25 °C until\nL477@P4: green leaves formed and acclimated under 16h light/8h darkness at 22 °C in soil\nL478@P4: consisting of peat moss and perlite at a ratio of 2 to 1.\nL479@P4: Tissue clearing. Leaves were fixed in 20% (v/v) acetic acid (Wako) dissolved in\nL480@P4: ethanol (Wako) with four 15-min rounds of decompression to −0.08MPa in gauge\nL481@P4: pressure at room temperature and then kept overnight at 4 °C. Fixed leaves were\nL482@P4: rehydrated using a dilution series of ethanol (90%, 70%, 50%, 30% and 0%) and\nL483@P4: cleared in a solution consisting of 8 g of chloral hydrate (Wako), 1ml of glycerol\nL484@P4: (Wako) and 2ml of distilled water for 3d at room temperature under continuous\nL485@P4: dark conditions.\nL486@P4: Microscopy. Stereoscopic images and videos were taken using a SZX16 (Olympus)\nL487@P4: fluorescence microscope equipped with a SZX2-FGFP long-pass filter (Olympus)\nL488@P4: and a digital camera DP74 (Olympus). Fluorescence videos were taken using\nL489@P4: the linear mode of cellSens Standard (1.17) in a range where the fluorescence\nL490@P4: intensities of samples and brightness values of captured images are linear. All\nL491@P4: fluorescence movies were taken at 50 frames per second, with a 2-ms exposure\nL492@P4: time (Fig. 2a, b) or 15-ms exposure time (others) and 16× gain, in linear contrast\nL493@P4: mode and with 2×2 binning. Images were saved as a sequence of uncompressed\nL494@P4: tiff files. Blue, green and red channels were split from the original images and only\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNational Institute for Basic Biology / News (https://www.nibb.ac.jp/en/pressroom/news/2020/10/06.html)\nciteturn25view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn16view0\",\"pattern\":\"brain\"}); Total lines: 274\nL0: # cite0†National Institutes of Natural Sciences National Institute for Basic Biology L1: \nL2:   * cite1†Access L3:   * cite2†Contact us L4:   * cite3†For Media L5:   *   * cite4†JP / cite0†EN L6: \nL7: [Input: Search Text] [Input]\nL8: \nL9: Menu\nL10: \nL11: cite0†HOME L12: \nL13: About NIBB\nL14:     cite5†Outline of NIBB cite6†Message From the Director-general cite7†Advisory Committee cite8†History cite9†Public Cooperation cite10†NIBB Photo Guide cite11†Research Projects cite12†Donations cite13†Encouraging Diversity cite1†Access cite2†Contact cite14†Accommodation cite15†Photo Gallery cite16†Video Gallery cite17†Recruitment L15: \nL16: News\nL17: \nL18:     cite18†News cite19†Press Release cite20†Publications L19: \nL20: NIBB Departments\nL21: \nL22:     cite21†Departments cite22